                    adoption_sub:           `Line managers actually use it (0–100)`,
                    roi_label:              `Net ROI / yr`,
                    roi_sub:                `Attrition avoided minus cost, in R$`,
                    budget_label:           `Frozen HR budget committed`,

                    clock_r1:               `90 days · turnover 24% vs ~15% norm`,
                    clock_r2:               `Frame the question · pick ONE intervention`,
                    clock_r3:               `R$22M frozen · prove causation`,
                    clock_r4:               `Defend the ROI to the CFO`,

                    round_name_r1:          `Mine the Data`,
                    round_name_r2:          `Question & Intervention`,
                    round_name_r3:          `Allocate & Measure`,
                    round_name_r4:          `Commit & Defend`,

                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Mine the data and find the real driver`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Move from anecdote to evidence. Leadership says ‘we have an engagement problem,’ but you hold five years of turnover, performance, manager-quality and project-utilisation data nobody has ever mined. <strong>Segment the attrition</strong> to find WHERE and WHO the firm is actually losing — and resist the loud, firm-wide story. The data point to a narrow segment: high performers on chronically <strong>over-utilised projects under weak managers</strong>, roughly 600 people. Identify the factors that travel with regretted exits and, critically, <em>separate plausible causes from mere correlates</em> before you spend a single real on a coincidence.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Pick the right question and the intervention`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Choose the people-analytics <strong>question</strong> worth answering — a sharp, decision-relevant one beats the popular ‘raise engagement.’ Then select <strong>one intervention</strong> and state the causal mechanism by which it should move the metric. Four compete for the frozen budget: a firm-wide engagement program (R$6M), a manager-capability program for the weakest 20% of managers (R$3M), a project-staffing rebalance (R$2M), and across-the-board retention bonuses (R$8M). The <em>popular bonus buys a pause, not a fix</em>; the cheaper manager and staffing levers address the actual driver. Flag the adoption risk now — an intervention managers won't use has zero ROI.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Allocate the frozen budget and build the ROI case`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Allocate within the frozen <strong>R$22M</strong> envelope and — this is the price of a credible ROI — fund a <strong>measurement design</strong> (a control group or staggered rollout) so you can prove causation rather than assert it. Set a defensible <strong>target attrition reduction</strong>: each point cut across ~400 regretted exits is worth roughly R$3M avoided. Resist splitting the budget across all four programs — <em>diffuse spend proves nothing</em> and recreates exactly what the CFO froze. Build the ROI in finance's language, with a base/low/high sensitivity range.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Commit, drive adoption and defend the ROI`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Present to the CFO and a business-unit leader. Design the <strong>manager-adoption mechanics</strong> that win skeptical line managers — make billability the ally, not the enemy. Name the <strong>leading indicators</strong> you will track before the lagging ROI lands, and commit to building HR's analytics capability so this keeps happening. The CFO will press on whether you <em>spread the spend or focused it</em>, and whether your effect is causation or coincidence. An elegant intervention nobody adopts has zero ROI; defend the single-intervention focus with a control group and an R$ number.`,

                    r1_segment_label:       `Segment the turnover data. Where is the firm actually losing high performers?`,
                    r1_driver_label:        `Which factors travel most strongly with regretted high-performer exits?`,
                    r1_rigor_label:         `How hard do you separate plausible causes from mere correlates before acting? (%)`,
                    r1_rigor_readout:       `Discipline in telling cause from correlation:`,
                    seg_overutil:           `High performers on chronically over-utilised projects under weak managers (~600 people)`,
                    seg_firmwide:           `Everywhere — it is a firm-wide engagement problem across all 4,200 people`,
                    seg_juniors:            `Junior staff in their first year — normal early churn`,
                    seg_pay:                `Anyone underpaid versus the market — it is purely a compensation gap`,
                    drv_utilmanager:        `Chronic over-utilisation plus weak managers — the likely causal driver`,
                    drv_engagement:         `Low engagement scores — the survey everyone already looks at`,
                    drv_tenure:             `Tenure alone — people just leave around year three`,
                    drv_random:             `Nothing consistent — exits look essentially random`,

                    r2_question_label:      `Frame the single people-analytics question worth answering.`,
                    r2_intervention_label:  `Choose ONE intervention to test — the budget funds at most one or two.`,
                    r2_mechanism_label:     `State the causal mechanism: why should this move the metric?`,
                    r2_adoptionrisk_label:  `How candidly do you flag manager adoption risk now, not later? (%)`,
                    r2_adoptionrisk_readout:`Up-front attention to whether managers will use it:`,
                    q_narrow:               `How do we cut regretted attrition among high performers on over-utilised projects with weak managers?`,
                    q_engagement:           `How do we raise engagement across the whole firm?`,
                    q_benchmark:            `How do we get our turnover number down to the 15% industry benchmark?`,
                    q_dashboard:            `Which twelve HR metrics should the new dashboard display?`,
                    int_manager:            `Manager-capability program for the weakest 20% of managers (R$3M) — addresses the cause`,
                    int_staffing:           `Project-staffing / utilisation rebalance (R$2M) — relieves the over-utilisation driver`,
                    int_engagement:         `Firm-wide engagement program for all 4,200 (R$6M) — treats the whole body`,
                    int_bonus:              `Across-the-board retention bonuses (R$8M) — popular, symptom-level`,
                    mech_cause:             `It removes the actual driver — better managers and saner utilisation, so high performers stop leaving`,
                    mech_correlation:       `Engaged employees stay, so anything that lifts engagement should lift retention`,
                    mech_buy:               `More money makes people stay — pay them to not leave`,

                    r3_measurement_label:   `Design the measurement that lets you claim CAUSATION, not coincidence.`,
                    r3_focus_label:         `How do you spend the frozen R$22M envelope?`,
                    r3_target_label:        `Target attrition reduction in the segment, in percentage points (current 24%).`,
                    r3_target_hint:         `Each point cut across ~400 regretted exits is worth roughly R$3M avoided. Be ambitious but defensible — a wild claim the CFO won't believe scores nothing.`,
                    r3_sensitivity_label:   `Build a base/low/high sensitivity range into the ROI case? (%)`,
                    r3_sensitivity_readout: `Rigour of the sensitivity range you show finance:`,
                    meas_staggered:         `Staggered rollout with a control group — treated vs untreated business units compared`,
                    meas_prepost:           `Before-and-after on everyone at once — compare this year's attrition to last year's`,
                    meas_none:              `No design — just report the attrition number and attribute the change to us`,
                    focus_focused:          `Fund the one chosen intervention plus the measurement design — defund the rest`,
                    focus_twolever:         `Fund the two cause-addressing levers (manager + staffing) plus measurement`,
                    focus_spread:           `Split the budget thinly across all four programs to keep everyone happy`,

                    r4_adoption_label:      `Design the manager-adoption mechanics. How do you win skeptical line managers?`,
                    r4_leading_label:       `What leading indicators will you track before the lagging ROI lands?`,
                    r4_pitch_label:         `The CFO presses: defend the single-intervention focus and the causal claim.`,
                    r4_capability_label:    `Commit to building HR's analytics capability so this keeps happening? (%)`,
                    adopt_billability:      `Make billability the ally — show managers it protects their billable team and staffing`,
                    adopt_train:            `Run a mandatory training session and assume they will comply afterwards`,
                    adopt_mandate:          `Mandate it from the top and audit managers who don't follow the new process`,
                    adopt_none:             `Roll it out and trust that a good analytical design sells itself`,
                    lead_leading:           `Utilisation balance and manager-quality scores in the target segment — early signal of effect`,
                    lead_lagging:           `Only the annual attrition number — wait twelve months to see if it moved`,
                    lead_vanity:            `Program attendance and satisfaction scores — easy to report, weak as proof`,
                    pitch_focus:            `One causal intervention, a control group proving the effect, and an R$ ROI with a range`,
                    pitch_hedge:            `We spread the spend so something is bound to work — we can't isolate which`,
                    pitch_correlation:      `Engaged people stay, so our engagement spend will obviously cut attrition`,

                    warn_rigor_range:       `Your cause-vs-correlation discipline must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_adoptionrisk_range:`Your adoption-risk attention must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_target_range:      `Target attrition reduction must be between 0 and 15 percentage points.`,
                    warn_sensitivity_range: `Your sensitivity rigour must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_capability_range:  `Your capability commitment must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_segment_right: `<strong>That's the right segmentation.</strong> You ignored the firm-wide story and located regretted attrition in the ~600 high performers on over-utilised projects under weak managers. You're treating a localised infection, not dosing 4,200 people. Now find the driver.`,
                    coach_r1_firmwide:      `<strong>You've accepted leadership's framing.</strong> ‘A firm-wide engagement problem’ points you at 4,200 people when your own data concentrate regretted attrition in maybe 600. What question would have pointed you at the 600 instead of the whole body?`,
                    coach_r1_pay:           `<strong>Pay is the easy story.</strong> But your best people on stable projects with good managers stay at market pay; the ones on over-utilised projects with weak managers leave. The data say the driver isn't mainly compensation. Look again.`,
                    coach_r1_juniors:       `<strong>First-year churn is expected and cheap.</strong> The R$180k-per-exit pain is in your high performers, not your juniors. You've segmented to the cheapest exits, not the costliest ones. Re-segment toward where the billings bleed.`,
                    coach_r1_engagement:    `<strong>Engagement is the correlate everyone already watches.</strong> Engaged employees do stay — but that doesn't prove engagement causes retention. The data suggest over-utilisation and weak managers drive the regretted exits. Don't spend millions on a correlation.`,
                    coach_r1_random:        `<strong>Exits are not random.</strong> Saying so is how you justify doing nothing — or doing the popular thing. Your data show a clear pattern around utilisation and manager quality. Mine it.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You located the segment AND named the likely causal driver — over-utilisation plus weak managers, not the engagement survey. That's the foundation that lets you ask the right question and choose an intervention that addresses a cause.`,
                    coach_r1_lowrigor:      `<strong>You're skipping the cause-vs-correlation discipline.</strong> Without separating plausible causes from mere correlates you'll fund whatever is loudest — and the CFO will ask how you know it was your program and not luck. Tighten the analysis.`,
                    coach_r2_narrow:        `<strong>That's the right question.</strong> Narrow, causal and decision-relevant — it points you straight at the 600 and an intervention you can measure. ‘Raise engagement’ would have pointed at the whole firm and proved nothing.`,
                    coach_r2_engagementq:   `<strong>That's the tempting wrong question.</strong> ‘Raise engagement across the firm’ is popular and vague — it sends you at 4,200 people and a R$6M program you can't tie to retention. Ask the narrow question your segmentation earned you.`,
                    coach_r2_benchmark:     `<strong>A benchmark is a target, not a question.</strong> ‘Get to 15%’ doesn't tell you who to act on or why they leave. Frame the causal question about the segment, then the number follows.`,
                    coach_r2_dashboard:     `<strong>I don't fund dashboards.</strong> ‘Which twelve metrics to display’ is reporting, not a decision. Analytics that doesn't end in a chosen action and an R$ number is just a screen. What are you going to DO?`,
                    coach_r2_manager:       `<strong>The manager-capability program addresses the cause.</strong> Weak managers drive your regretted exits; fixing the weakest 20% at R$3M is cheaper than the bonus and targets the actual driver. Now prove the effect.`,
                    coach_r2_staffing:      `<strong>The staffing rebalance attacks over-utilisation directly</strong> — at R$2M it's your cheapest cause-addressing lever, and managers feel it in their own billable teams. Strong, adoptable choice.`,
                    coach_r2_engagementint: `<strong>A firm-wide engagement program treats the whole body for a localised infection.</strong> R$6M across 4,200 people, when ~600 are leaving for over-utilisation and weak managers. You're chasing the correlate, not the cause.`,
                    coach_r2_bonus:         `<strong>The retention bonus is the popular, obvious spend — and your biggest.</strong> R$8M across the board buys a pause, not a fix: people leaving for a bad manager take the bonus and leave next year. Did you pick the intervention that CAUSES retention, or the one that's easiest to approve?`,
                    coach_r2_mechcorrelation:`<strong>‘Engaged employees stay’ is a correlation, not a mechanism.</strong> It won't survive the CFO asking how you know it's your program and not the market. Name the causal chain — better managers and saner utilisation, so high performers stop leaving.`,
                    coach_r2_mechbuy:       `<strong>‘Pay them to stay’ is buying the symptom.</strong> Money pauses the exit without touching why they wanted to leave. The bonus recipients with bad managers leave next cycle. Address the cause instead.`,
                    coach_r2_lowadoptionrisk:`<strong>You're treating adoption as a footnote.</strong> Line managers think HR doesn't understand billability. If you don't flag and design for their buy-in now, your elegant intervention earns zero in the real world.`,
                    coach_r3_staggered:     `<strong>A staggered rollout with a control group is the price of a credible ROI.</strong> Comparing treated vs untreated business units lets you answer the CFO's ‘how do you know it was your program?’ — that's causation, not coincidence.`,
                    coach_r3_prepost:       `<strong>Before-and-after on everyone is weak evidence.</strong> If attrition falls, was it your program, the market, or luck? With no control group you can't isolate the effect — and the CFO knows it. Stagger the rollout.`,
                    coach_r3_nomeasure:     `<strong>No measurement design means no defensible ROI.</strong> You're telling the CFO attrition will fall because engaged employees stay. She asks: ‘How will you know it was your program and not the market?’ Correlation isn't causation — and it isn't ROI.`,
                    coach_r3_focused:       `<strong>Focus is the condition for provable ROI.</strong> One intervention plus a measurement design means you can defend exactly what worked — instead of the diffuse spend the CFO already froze.`,
                    coach_r3_twolever:      `<strong>The two cause-addressing levers together still defend cleanly.</strong> Manager capability plus the staffing rebalance both attack the driver, fit inside the frozen budget, and managers feel the staffing relief directly. A focused, provable bet.`,
                    coach_r3_spread:        `<strong>You've split R$22M across all four programs.</strong> A little engagement, a little manager training, some staffing, partial bonuses — now you can't prove any single one worked, and I see the same diffuse spend I froze. The mandate was ONE intervention you can defend.`,
                    coach_r3_lowsensitivity:`<strong>No sensitivity range reads as overconfidence.</strong> Finance trusts a base/low/high estimate more than a single hopeful number. Show the range and the assumptions behind it.`,
                    coach_r4_billability:   `<strong>Making billability the ally is how adoption actually happens.</strong> Show managers the intervention protects their billable team and eases staffing, and the ‘HR doesn't understand billability’ wall comes down. That's adoption designed in, not bolted on.`,
                    coach_r4_train:         `<strong>A training session is not an adoption plan.</strong> Skeptical managers attend, nod, and go back to their way. Adoption comes from making their billable life better today — not from a mandatory afternoon.`,
                    coach_r4_mandate:       `<strong>Mandate-and-audit breeds compliance, not adoption.</strong> Order managers who already distrust HR to follow a new process and they'll do the minimum. An intervention they resent earns a fraction of its potential ROI.`,
                    coach_r4_noadopt:       `<strong>A good design does not sell itself.</strong> Roll out and three months later the managers haven't engaged — they still think HR doesn't understand billability. Zero adoption, zero ROI, no matter how elegant the model.`,
                    coach_r4_lagging:       `<strong>Waiting twelve months for the attrition number is flying blind.</strong> If adoption is slipping you won't know until it's too late. Track leading indicators — utilisation balance and manager quality — so you see the effect coming.`,
                    coach_r4_vanity:        `<strong>Attendance and satisfaction are vanity metrics.</strong> They're easy to report and prove nothing about retention. Track the leading indicators that actually predict the lagging ROI.`,
                    coach_r4_focus:         `<strong>That's a board-ready defence.</strong> One causal intervention, a control group proving the effect, manager adoption designed in, and an R$ ROI with a range. That earns HR the seat — and the budget — back.`,
                    coach_r4_hedge:         `<strong>‘We spread it so something will work’ is exactly what I froze.</strong> If you can't isolate which lever worked, you can't defend any of it. The whole point was one intervention you can prove.`,
                    coach_r4_pitchcorrelation:`<strong>You've defended a correlation to the CFO.</strong> ‘Engaged people stay so engagement spend cuts attrition’ — with no control group, that's coincidence dressed as ROI. I'm cutting the budget 15% and reallocating it.`,

